This was my very first ascension - it feels good to finally make it.
Just for the record, it was with 6363966 points.  At my peak I had AC
-24, HP 364 Pwr 269 Str 18/**

So what did I do that made it easy?
 - lots of genocides - basically 3 blessed scrolls to take out all
mind-flayers, vampires and eel-like things. That was basically a full
wand of wishing right there.
 - lots of sacrificing on aligned altars (and converted a few to get
more).
 - paid lots of protection money to priests, including everything I
looted from Fort Ludios.
 - finally learned (late in the game) about how to use foocubuses.
 - didn't use a single healing-type potion unless I was at full health
 - ate a lot of giants.
 - wore a ring of conflict pretty much non-stop for the last half of
the game. (No, not many pets.)
 - I've seen very few samurai make it. I guess if I'd known that at
the start I might have played a different character.
 - got as good as I could at spellcasting. Nothing worked more than
42% of the time, but learned over 30 spells. (Gotta love fireball,
cone of cold, speed self and charm monster)
 - 3 luckstones (just in case)

What did I do that might have made it harder?
 - polymorphed no objects.
 - wished for no artifacts (Still managed to get Sunsword (+0),
Firebrand (+1), Excalibur (duh!) (+4), Snickersnee (+4 but never
used), Tsurugi of Muramasa (+4), Dragonbane (+0, almost never used),
Werebane (+0, just for demons)) Mostly just slugged away with
Excalibur and my Tsurugi. Maybe I should have boosted Firebrand
instead...
 - no metal armor - decided to maximize my spellcasting. Mostly used
+5 gray dragon, +5 hawaiian shirt, +1 cloak of protection, +2 boots of
stealth.
 - no launchers - gave up on arrows/ya's early and focused on spells
and wands for ranged attacks.
 - no life-saving amulets - amulet of reflection was just too sweet.
 - almost no pets. Ring of conflict too sweet. On the Astral plane I
could wade through crowded rooms taking basically no damage aside from
the three riders.
 - Lost about 100 Max HP from Rider of Death until I realized that I
should really wield Excalibur when he's next to me. (Doh!)
 - eventually paid off all the (living) shopkeepers I'd stolen from,
just out of politeness.


